{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-07-fractions-numerator-denominator",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Fractions: Numerator and Denominator Equations",
    "description": "Treat numerator and denominator as separate unknowns and translate every change to them before simplifying.",
    "teachingApproach": "Make numerator and denominator visible as separate quantities from the first screen. Progress through equal changes, two independent transformed-fraction clues, fixed numerator-denominator gaps, one-sided changes, percentage changes, reciprocal quadratics, product/quotient relations and a ratio-based capstone. Each block emphasizes exactly what changed before any simplification.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach an unknown fraction as x/y and distinguish changing the fraction from changing its numerator or denominator. Cover adding or subtracting values from both parts, a fixed difference or ratio between numerator and denominator, two changed-fraction clues, percentage increases to numerator and denominator, reciprocal-minus-fraction conditions, and product/quotient information for a pair of fractions. Build equations before simplification and check the final fraction in every stated transformation. Use positive proper-fraction conditions where needed to choose the valid root."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's solve fraction puzzles without mixing up the fraction with its numerator and denominator.\n\nA small wording change—'add 1 to the fraction' versus 'add 1 to numerator and denominator'—creates completely different math. We'll keep those parts separate from the start.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For an unknown fraction, keep numerator and denominator separate.\n\nCall the fraction x/y.\n\nIf 1 is added to both parts, the new fraction is (x+1)/(y+1).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "That is very different from adding 1 to the value x/y.\n\nExample: 3/5 becomes 4/6 when 1 is added to numerator and denominator. It does not become 8/5.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "A fraction is x/y. If 2 is added to both numerator and denominator, which expression is correct?",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "input": {
        "type": "CHOICE",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "label": "x/y + 2"
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "label": "(x+2)/(y+2)"
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "label": "(x+2)/y"
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "label": "x/(y+2)"
          }
        ]
      },
